n/bdaf9f6cd83b716006350d9fcf604ade1db193e95a5a56ab00a9dd1a9cf617e8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-93827" :question/id #uuid "4f5f62bb-4839-53ca-a27c-2aa63221765a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93827" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-93827/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-93827"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-7903"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=25{x}^{2}+5x-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -10(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n25\\alpha {x}^{2}+(25\\beta -20\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}25\\alpha =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 25\\beta -20\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-64206"] :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-77763/selection/d7e5ae51cbc295e7ffcad50b8a378065ade253c2d146bcafd4c8e9e7c8b8b1a6" :answer/id #uuid "79c79637-df5f-5809-9e49-8288f6e33f8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+3x-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77763/selection/7ea5587e90407b82ab4e86dc3b3442a29a62696c8d60399d74c4c7feb945e426" :answer/id #uuid "2be27aa9-ef75-565e-b292-c7f21b79b5c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x+8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77763/selection/11b803500c17ea2d06094694c24b56b413176ff3173a8e17a41dcfb9f09b03a5" :answer/id #uuid "6d447568-45b8-5b7c-8832-0f6d851ef316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+2x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77763/selection/72f78624aa3d5a1fc3440c512b5feca5a96b2456ea751edf8c453ab549347c83" :answer/id #uuid "08c9aef4-21e2-5286-b9da-f008dbd978c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77763/selection/6a916a1ef1152173623a358059c8c3e191e62dec5b37160bbd14c3e1d7c5e2ad" :answer/id #uuid "d55dc3e8-72e1-57dc-96a7-88babd6a6ee2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77763/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6bda93-ff1b-5f34-b8be-63689be1468f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-77763/selection/6a916a1ef1152173623a358059c8c3e191e62dec5b37160bbd14c3e1d7c5e2ad" :answer-field/choices ["q-77763/selection/d7e5ae51cbc295e7ffcad50b8a378065ade253c2d146bcafd4c8e9e7c8b8b1a6" "q-77763/selection/7ea5587e90407b82ab4e86dc3b3442a29a62696c8d60399d74c4c7feb945e426" "q-77763/selection/11b803500c17ea2d06094694c24b56b413176ff3173a8e17a41dcfb9f09b03a5" "q-77763/selection/72f78624aa3d5a1fc3440c512b5feca5a96b2456ea751edf8c453ab549347c83" "q-77763/selection/6a916a1ef1152173623a358059c8c3e191e62dec5b37160bbd14c3e1d7c5e2ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77763" :question/id #uuid "cd702971-4dc4-5e03-9b53-1243b162ebed" :question/math-academy-id "q-77763" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -3(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\beta -6\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\beta -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =6,\\,\\gamma =6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-77763/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77763"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-7901"] :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{4}{3},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=0,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{4}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, $A=1.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(B-3A){e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nB-3A=5.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=8.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94258/selection/eca55b07c07e38b0e74a6d714023b8bf4d826163bfe28cbcd0a5731c27dd3c6b" :answer/id #uuid "51c78d6c-da3f-5410-ab1b-5e077c8d9137" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94258/selection/abc6fd572e2e34c7b52a958d56b7394ed9dbe374df84933e653e2bfa7d2c4550" :answer/id #uuid "bf3cdf9b-3d66-5d51-9de6-a7fa25ff6636" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94258/selection/c721e7a3a960c891e390a34c0c9dce8ebbbe2b4f72425eeb988945d83c0b577d" :answer/id #uuid "8ac6096c-93f8-56db-9fa8-b8b9aa5b2acf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94258/selection/20eebccecf43cfc9e5cbdf64d3cc2994554343ffa88acf7509ccb7b8c1c44da1" :answer/id #uuid "357a16fa-ff1c-530e-97f2-a5b05fdcef3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94258/selection/61d7cb5921b4ef41c6036fd55681cd4fe2727f3139fb50dd0c70911801569084" :answer/id #uuid "eee5fc65-ec89-5a41-9530-5ac0e5ea3a5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94258/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca1551c-52cb-5cc5-a813-2ff731a48319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94258/selection/c721e7a3a960c891e390a34c0c9dce8ebbbe2b4f72425eeb988945d83c0b577d" :answer-field/choices ["q-94258/selection/eca55b07c07e38b0e74a6d714023b8bf4d826163bfe28cbcd0a5731c27dd3c6b" "q-94258/selection/abc6fd572e2e34c7b52a958d56b7394ed9dbe374df84933e653e2bfa7d2c4550" "q-94258/selection/c721e7a3a960c891e390a34c0c9dce8ebbbe2b4f72425eeb988945d83c0b577d" "q-94258/selection/20eebccecf43cfc9e5cbdf64d3cc2994554343ffa88acf7509ccb7b8c1c44da1" "q-94258/selection/61d7cb5921b4ef41c6036fd55681cd4fe2727f3139fb50dd0c70911801569084"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94258" :question/id #uuid "6e8bc97a-c1b6-51d3-8b5a-21e74de39d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-94258" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=-8\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=-8,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-4Bx{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4A+B & =0 \\\\ -4(1)+B & =0 \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94258/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94258"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94329/selection/ae7b447a1be78e80cf8d6af02741ca781d83dab13d1fa303b927a854c632de55" :answer/id #uuid "da0700a9-6f99-53d3-b167-d1e64e8ee804" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94329/selection/73786d153568ec40454a681a67024fdc28c2d9235b8e0367c729a595c6e5bcd6" :answer/id #uuid "cd1c9da6-b967-50bf-9702-1db746098c8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94329/selection/169b5020e2d21b318cae8e7b3afe46efc5616f57414bb075ea5760c468adb0cd" :answer/id #uuid "c04e5305-b37e-5b36-8f70-85112b13595e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94329/selection/053a26f92c24b0c5e1e3cf49577aa4a6e5a36d2a00bf4d228c82e72db5264daa" :answer/id #uuid "459789aa-4181-5d3a-892f-e2624d53e0e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94329/selection/8024a6b34d765d5fe66987f13b870e82a3bdde90a17ec9ad9dca46a912b6fe32" :answer/id #uuid "cbc004a1-86aa-57bb-947b-7074a44e84a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94329/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a725290-09ae-545b-a24c-b3d59324f0c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94329/selection/053a26f92c24b0c5e1e3cf49577aa4a6e5a36d2a00bf4d228c82e72db5264daa" :answer-field/choices ["q-94329/selection/ae7b447a1be78e80cf8d6af02741ca781d83dab13d1fa303b927a854c632de55" "q-94329/selection/73786d153568ec40454a681a67024fdc28c2d9235b8e0367c729a595c6e5bcd6" "q-94329/selection/169b5020e2d21b318cae8e7b3afe46efc5616f57414bb075ea5760c468adb0cd" "q-94329/selection/053a26f92c24b0c5e1e3cf49577aa4a6e5a36d2a00bf4d228c82e72db5264daa" "q-94329/selection/8024a6b34d765d5fe66987f13b870e82a3bdde90a17ec9ad9dca46a912b6fe32"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94329" :question/id #uuid "7fd3f68f-27a8-5cd0-8e1d-99989db25fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94329" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-1 & =0 \\\\ P & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}[(2Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-Q-2P)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]-2x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q-P+1 & =4 \\\\ 2Q-1+1 & =4 \\\\ Q & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94329/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94329"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94342/selection/7e57758844adbe2a5a3038ab8dcd2de1e39b830382e0acc1c4cbec51724c6932" :answer/id #uuid "511e2c7e-605f-5674-bdc3-740bbd612faa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94342/selection/2fe78e028b85eb4d0bbe94fc3c15f4301dcbbd6014423646231bd256b9032730" :answer/id #uuid "b116b9eb-10aa-5126-9ea9-db7b0e85b841" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}+3{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94342/selection/e5754c330cfdbb042f2c74c1b8155dac98550478f8065600e0ec2dc90ecae157" :answer/id #uuid "df55cf39-98a8-5b5a-8752-c5f8dc4c9697" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94342/selection/3dab206bec7a70588643e87ccec59a118a88494b0fe80145e018db62615ff2cc" :answer/id #uuid "265a4f54-42f0-546c-8f11-e24caf6c8f4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-2x}-{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94342/selection/c87073f156f2fe2421735df3443dc30abbf04fc29efbc0166a2cb5f42af0cfe5" :answer/id #uuid "da40b38c-1d5e-5e59-a088-3ee7966d66be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94342/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea13de8d-ef21-5941-9743-a6122dca0081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94342/selection/c87073f156f2fe2421735df3443dc30abbf04fc29efbc0166a2cb5f42af0cfe5" :answer-field/choices ["q-94342/selection/7e57758844adbe2a5a3038ab8dcd2de1e39b830382e0acc1c4cbec51724c6932" "q-94342/selection/2fe78e028b85eb4d0bbe94fc3c15f4301dcbbd6014423646231bd256b9032730" "q-94342/selection/e5754c330cfdbb042f2c74c1b8155dac98550478f8065600e0ec2dc90ecae157" "q-94342/selection/3dab206bec7a70588643e87ccec59a118a88494b0fe80145e018db62615ff2cc" "q-94342/selection/c87073f156f2fe2421735df3443dc30abbf04fc29efbc0166a2cb5f42af0cfe5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94342" :question/id #uuid "44df3166-ba97-53d9-8a68-878f2b446ce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94342" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y=8x-4\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}-2x+1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y=8x-4,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+1 & =4 \\\\ A+B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+2B{e}^{2x}-2,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2A+2B-2 & =0 \\\\ A-B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=3 \\\\ A-B=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}-2x+1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94342/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94342"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-8034"] :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=13{x}^{2}+15x-24,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral, ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+26(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n26\\alpha {x}^{2}+(26\\beta +4\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}26\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 26\\beta +4\\alpha =15\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$  \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP-1=2.\n$$\n\nThus, $P=3.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}[(5Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-(5P+Q)\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x]+x+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5Q-P+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=3$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6721"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} - \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y = 6.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = -1, 2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ {- x} + B e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0,$ which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0,$ or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-0-2\\alpha =6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = -3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6607"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} -4 \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} +4 y = 8x + 4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root $\\lambda =  2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =8x+4 \\\\ 4\\alpha x+(4\\beta -4\\alpha ) & =8x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\beta -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=2, \\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6608"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6786"] :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x} - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3,\n\\qquad y(0) =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\ \\ y'(0) = 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B -\\dfrac{1}{2}  = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nA-2B -3 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ A-2B=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x}  - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
